DISCUSSION = [
"Suspected dyslipidemia at screening was modestly more common than expected in a belt of southwestern districts and less common in the inland east, with a stable ranking across 7 years. Contrary to the prespecified hypotheses, it was higher where screening uptake and public sports facilities were higher. The uptake association arose within districts over time rather than between districts, and the sports facility estimate weakened with province-by-year effects. No consistent association with walking or moderate physical activity was found. Both credible associations were small, of similar size to that of a negative-control exposure.",
"### Screening reach and the meaning of a suspected judgement",
"A suspected judgement records dyslipidemia that is found at screening and not yet treated. Uptake was expected to lower it, on the reasoning that wider screening and care lead to earlier treatment, but the opposite was observed. The association appeared within districts in the same year, was absent with lags, was not positive for dyslipidemia already under treatment, and became uncertain when the pandemic years 2020-2021 were excluded, so it partly rests on the large swings in attendance during the COVID-19 pandemic. This pattern is compatible with case finding, in which years with wider attendance bring more people with previously unknown dyslipidemia to testing, as suggested by low awareness among younger Korean adults and the gap between a screening result and statin treatment [@lee2014serum; @ahn2015treatment]. Because expected counts were matched to the number and approximate age structure of examinees, the association concerns the proportion of examinees flagged, and it cannot be separated here from changes in the composition of attendees, in their treatment status, or in the approximation of their age structure. The interpretation is therefore post hoc.",
"### Physical activity and sports facilities",
"Exercise raises high-density lipoprotein cholesterol and lowers triglycerides in trials, and walkable neighborhoods have been associated with more favorable triglyceride and high-density lipoprotein levels [@kodama2007effect; @smart2025effect; @cakmak2024association]. No consistent association with walking or moderate physical activity was found here, and the walking estimate depended on how the district effect was modeled. Survey rates describe a different sample from screening attendees and are self-reported, which can bias district-level associations in either direction. The positive association with public sports facilities runs against the hypothesis and against a study in Gyeonggi Province in which older adults living farther from such facilities more often reported dyslipidemia [@lee2021objectively]. Facilities per resident are highest in sparsely populated, older districts; the association remained with facilities per area and with population density, but weakened with province-by-year effects and reversed under the earlier testing schedule. Rurality, population composition, regional changes over time, and other unmeasured factors could each contribute, and the analysis cannot distinguish them; the association should not be read as causal. Obesity, drinking, and smoking were not associated at the district level despite their individual effects on lipids [@vekic2019obesity; @craig1989cigarette; @rimm1999moderate], which may reflect self-report and the narrow range of these rates across districts.",
"### A regional rather than urban-rural pattern",
"Both hot and cold spots were mostly sparsely populated districts, so the pattern did not simply follow urbanization. Among older Korean patients with dyslipidemia, statin prescription was more common in rural provinces, including the Jeolla region, than in metropolitan cities [@han2021regional], and cardiovascular risk was higher in less densely populated regions [@han2021regional2]. A higher share of untreated dyslipidemia at screening in the same region is not inconsistent with more prescription among those diagnosed, because the 2 measures describe different people. Because Gi* hot spots describe local clusters and the excess was moderate, the map points to where follow-up of screening results could be examined further rather than to places with a markedly different burden.",
"### Strengths and limitations",
"The study covered every district for 7 years under one lipid testing schedule, standardized for sex and age, and tested associations with space-time interaction models, lags, and comparison outcomes from the same screening program. It has limitations. First, it is ecological, and associations do not describe individuals [@morgenstern1995ecologic]. Second, a suspected judgement rests on a single screening test, and laboratory and judgement practices may differ by region. Third, the examinee age structure of each district was approximated from resident population and national screening rates, and any error could correlate with uptake. Fourth, the change in the testing schedule in 2018 limits comparison with earlier years, and the main period includes the COVID-19 pandemic, when attendance changed. Fifth, 10 characteristics and many specifications were examined, Gi* clusters were not corrected for multiple testing, the outcome was selected for its spatial structure, and the 2 credible associations were of similar size to that of fires, a negative-control exposure, with small E-values, so residual confounding could explain them. Sixth, the district panel was compiled from public statistics; the outcome and 11 of the 15 modeled characteristics matched a reference district database cell by cell, and the denominator differed by at most 5 examinees in a few cells (Supplementary Table S2).",
]
CONCLUSION = ["Suspected dyslipidemia found at Korean health screening is modestly higher in southwestern districts and lower in the inland east, a pattern that has been stable since the lipid testing schedule changed in 2018. Its district counts rose in years when screening uptake rose, partly during the COVID-19 pandemic and compatible with case finding, and showed no consistent association with walking or moderate physical activity. These small associations may reflect residual confounding, and district counts of suspected dyslipidemia are best read together with screening reach and the follow-up of those flagged."]
